# Worktrees created by the EnterWorktree tool. Other sessions work here
# concurrently, and `make ci`'s `ruff check .` is unscoped, so without this
# one session's in-progress files fail another session's gate on code that is
# in neither commit. Measured 19 September 2026: a planted file here produced
# 4 errors in a clean tree.
.claude/worktrees/

# Scratch handoff for a local model picking this repo up when the Claude usage
# limit is hit. One file per repository, at this root, shared by every session
# and every worktree; a global hook in ~/.claude/hooks/handoff-local/ keeps its
# mechanical facts current. Untracked on purpose: it carries sessions'
# unverified working notes, which must never read as reviewed history. The glob
# also covers the `.tmp-<pid>` file the hook writes before renaming over it.
# The hook also deletes from that file, under one rule: a section goes only if
# nobody ever typed in it and its session could not be proved to exist against a
# transcript on disk. "Typed in it" means differing from the template in any way
# at all, including prose added beside an untouched placeholder rather than over
# it, because the first version of that test counted placeholders and deleted a
# section with two paragraphs of notes in it. Nothing a session wrote is ever
# removed, which is why the file is safe to leave to a hook and still not safe
# to commit.
/handoff-for-local.md*

# `dev-docs/` was ignored here until 26 September 2026, as "internal
# development documentation (not for public consumption)". It is tracked now.
# What it held: the three documents CLAUDE.md calls the architecture, and
# fourteen decision records -- 312K of the reasoning behind decisions the code
# cannot state, on one disk, with no second copy. A repository that carries
# `postern-mcp` as its published name will one day be public, and these should
# be reviewed before that happens; losing them to a disk failure in the
# meantime is the larger risk, and the one that was live.
# Scanned before tracking: no key material, no credential assignments, no
# personal addresses, and every hostname in them is `.test` or `.internal`.
